<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/14th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle of the Golden Spurs</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1302">1302</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> · Also known as Battle of Courtrai (1302) · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franco-flemish-war-1297-1305">Franco-Flemish War (1297–1305)</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle of the Golden Spurs was fought on 11 July 1302 at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kortrijk">Courtrai</a> (Kortrijk) in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/flanders">Flanders</a>, where an army of Flemish town militias fighting on foot destroyed a French cavalry army under Robert II of Artois. Hundreds of golden spurs taken from the dead knights were hung in a church at Courtrai. The victory showed that disciplined infantry could defeat mounted knights, and 11 July is the official holiday of Flanders.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-the-golden-spurs.jpg" alt="Battle of the Golden Spurs" width="320" height="251" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Battle_of_Courtrai2.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The battle belongs to the Franco-Flemish War of 1297–1305. King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-iv-of-france">Philip IV of France</a> hoped to reassert control over the County of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/flanders">Flanders</a>, a semi-independent polity notionally part of his kingdom, and perhaps to annex it. The French invaded in 1297 and again in 1300, and by May 1300 they controlled the county; Guy of Dampierre was imprisoned.</p><p>After Philip left, unrest returned in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bruges">Bruges</a> against the new governor, Jacques de Châtillon. On 18 May 1302 rebels who had fled the city came back and killed every Frenchman they could find, an act known as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/matins-of-bruges">Bruges Matins</a>. Most towns joined the rising, though <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghent">Ghent</a> did not, and most of the Flemish nobility took the French side.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>Philip sent a force under Count Robert II of Artois, built around a core of 2,500 noble cavalry. The Flemish army, led in the field by William of Jülich, was mostly town militia organised by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guild">guild</a>, between 8,000 and 10,000 infantry. They carried pikes, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crossbow">crossbows</a> and the goedendag, a thick wooden shaft topped with a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/steel">steel</a> spike. The Flemish besieged the castle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kortrijk">Courtrai</a> from 26 June and fought the French on 11 July beside the Groeninge stream.</p><p>The ground was crossed by ditches and streams that the Flemish had dug and in places hidden, which robbed the French charge of momentum. Artois sent his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heavy-cavalry">heavy cavalry</a> across the brooks. The Flemish pikemen did not flinch, and many knights were knocked from their horses and killed with the goedendag. Artois ordered his rearguard forward, but it turned back to protect the baggage. The count was cut down, and the surviving knights were driven into the marshes and slaughtered. The Flemish took few prisoners for ransom.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kortrijk">Courtrai</a> castle surrendered on 13 July. The patrician regimes in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghent">Ghent</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ypres">Ypres</a> were overthrown and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guild">guilds</a> were officially recognised. The Annals of Ghent put French losses at more than 1,000 men, including 75 important nobles. The 500 pairs of spurs taken from the dead were given to the Church of Our Lady. The French soon reversed the result, winning at Mons-en-Pévèle in 1304, and the Peace of Athis-sur-Orge in 1305 made the Flemish pay substantial tribute.</p><p>Later writers saw the battle as an early sign of an infantry revolution, copied or adapted at Bannockburn, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-crecy">Crécy</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-agincourt">Agincourt</a>. In the 19th and 20th centuries it became the centre of a cult in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/flanders">Flanders</a>: Hendrik Conscience's 1838 novel The Lion of Flanders made it known, and in 1973 its date was made the official holiday of the Flemish Community.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Battle of the Golden Spurs” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…1300, the townsmen of Bruges rose in the Bruges Matins of May 1302, and on 11 July 1302 an army of Flemish militias, fighting on foot with pikes and the spiked club called the goedendag, destroyed the French cavalry at the <b>Battle of the Golden Spurs</b> near Courtrai.</blockquote><a href="/empire/county-of-flanders">County of Flanders · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The revolt led to the Flemish victory at the <b>Battle of the Golden Spurs</b> in July.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/may-18">18 May in history</a></li></ul></section>
